        </div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Best Answer Hub Playbooks · AI Skills</span>
        <div class="cav">It is the law now</div>
        <h1>AI Literacy Is Now a <em>Legal Duty</em> in the EU</h1>
        <p class="dek">Since February 2025, the EU AI Act requires organizations to make sure their staff understand AI. It is a duty on employers, not a test you pass, and it deliberately requires no certificate. Here is what Article 4 actually says, the myths around it, and what it means for you.</p>
        <div class="byline"><span><b>Best Answer Hub</b></span><span>~10 min read</span><span>Evidence-based</span><span>No signup</span></div>
        <div class="bandstrip">
          <div class="seg b1">Beginner<small>0–49</small></div>
          <div class="seg b2">Intermediate<small>50–79</small></div>
          <div class="seg b3">Advanced<small>80–100</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2025</div><div class="l">the year AI literacy became a legal duty in the EU</div><div class="src">EU AI Act, Article 4</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">39%</div><div class="l">of workplace AI users have had any AI training</div><div class="src">Microsoft &amp; LinkedIn, 2024</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">66%</div><div class="l">of leaders would not hire without AI skills</div><div class="src">Microsoft &amp; LinkedIn, 2024</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">56%</div><div class="l">wage premium for jobs that require AI skills</div><div class="src">PwC, 2025</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <div class="t">On this page</div>
        <a href="#what">What Article 4 says</a>
        <a href="#who">Who it applies to</a>
        <a href="#myths">What it does not require</a>
        <a href="#you">You vs your employer</a>
        <a href="#matters">Why it matters to you</a>
        <a href="#find">Find your level</a>
        <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
      </nav>

      <article>
        <p class="lead">As of 2 February 2025, AI literacy is no longer just a good idea in the EU, it is the law. <strong>Article 4 of the EU AI Act requires any organization that builds or uses AI to ensure its staff have a sufficient level of AI literacy.</strong> Crucially, the law puts the duty on employers, not individuals, and it deliberately requires no exam and no certificate. That makes this a useful moment to understand where you actually stand.</p>

        <p>This guide sticks closely to the European Commission's own words, because the rule is widely misreported. It pairs with the companion guides on <a href="/playbooks/how-good-is-your-ai-knowledge/">how good your AI knowledge really is</a> and the levels of AI users.</p>

        <div class="cav">The actual rule</div>
        <h2 id="what">What does Article 4 actually say?</h2>
        <p>It requires "providers and deployers of AI systems" to "take measures to ensure, to their best extent, a sufficient level of AI literacy of their staff and other persons dealing with the operation and use of AI systems on their behalf," judged against their knowledge, the context, and the risks (<a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 4</a>). The Act defines AI literacy plainly, in Article 3(56), as the "skills, knowledge and understanding" to make informed use of AI and to be aware of its opportunities, risks, and possible harms. In other words: knowing what AI can do, where it fails, and when to trust it.</p>

        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">The EU AI Act timeline</figcaption>
          <div class="timeline">
            <div class="tl"><span class="when">Aug 2024</span><div class="ev"><b>The EU AI Act enters into force.</b> A phased rollout begins, with different rules applying on different dates.</div></div>
            <div class="tl"><span class="when">2 Feb 2025</span><div class="ev"><b>Article 4 applies.</b> Organizations using AI must ensure staff AI literacy. Banned AI practices are also prohibited from this date.</div></div>
            <div class="tl"><span class="when">From Aug 2026</span><div class="ev"><b>Enforcement begins.</b> National market surveillance authorities start supervising and enforcing the rules, with penalties set by each member state.</div></div>
            <div class="tl"><span class="when">2026 to 2027</span><div class="ev"><b>High-risk rules phase in.</b> Stricter obligations for higher-risk AI systems take effect on a longer timeline.</div></div>
          </div>
          <p class="chart-src">Source: EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) and European Commission guidance. Article 4 has applied since 2 February 2025.</p>
        </figure>

        <div class="cav">Broader than you think</div>
        <h2 id="who">Who does it apply to?</h2>
        <p>Almost any organization that touches AI. The duty covers both providers (who build AI) and deployers (who use it), across all sectors and all risk levels, not just high-risk systems. The European Commission gives a deliberately everyday example: a company whose employees use ChatGPT to write advertising copy or translate text is in scope, and those staff "should be informed about the specific risks, for example hallucination" (<a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/ai-literacy-questions-answers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">European Commission, 2025</a>).</p>
        <div class="callout"><div class="h">It reaches beyond the EU</div><p>The rule is not limited to European companies. The Commission confirms it applies to organizations outside the EU when their AI system is placed on the EU market, used in the EU, or affects people in the EU. A non-EU employer whose AI touches EU users can fall within Article 4.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Clearing up the noise</div>
        <h2 id="myths">What the law does not require</h2>
        <p>Article 4 is widely misreported, often to sell training or certificates. Here is what it actually does not demand, in the Commission's own words.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>The myth</th><th>The truth</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">You need a certificate or to pass an exam</td><td>False. The Commission says "there is no need for a certificate" and no duty to measure staff knowledge</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Only tech companies are covered</td><td>False. It imposes no sector-specific requirements; any organization using AI is included</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Fines reach €35 million</td><td>False. That tier is for banned AI practices, not Article 4; its penalties are set by each member state</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">It only applies to EU companies</td><td>False. It can reach non-EU organizations whose AI affects people in the EU</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Employees can be fined</td><td>False. The duty falls on employers, not individual staff</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">One change to watch</div><p>In November 2025 the European Commission proposed amendments, in a package called the Digital Omnibus, that would shift more of the AI-literacy promotion role onto member states. It is a proposal, not yet law, so the duty as written still applies, and training for human oversight of high-risk AI would remain regardless.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Where you fit in</div>
        <h2 id="you">What it means for you, and for your employer</h2>
        <p>The legal weight sits with your employer. For you, the duty is less a rule to obey than a signal: AI literacy is now expected, and the people who have it are better placed. That matters, because most workers are still self-taught.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Your employer</th><th>You</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Who the law binds</td><td>Providers and deployers of AI</td><td>Not bound; you are the staff it protects</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">The obligation</td><td>Ensure staff have sufficient AI literacy</td><td>None legally, but expected to keep up</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">A certificate or exam</td><td>Not required to test staff</td><td>No EU credential exists to earn</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">If it is ignored</td><td>Possible enforcement from August 2026</td><td>Risk is to your competitiveness, not a fine</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Bottom line</td><td>A compliance duty</td><td>A career advantage</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">The law is catching up to a self-taught workforce</figcaption>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 600 280" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: 75% of knowledge workers use AI at work, but only 39% of workplace AI users have received any AI training">
            <defs><linearGradient id="gapgrad" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1"><stop offset="0" stop-color="#C6603F"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#8a4429"/></linearGradient></defs>
            <line x1="70" y1="232" x2="560" y2="232" stroke="#E1D4be" stroke-width="2"/>
            <rect x="150" y="78" width="120" height="154" rx="8" fill="url(#gapgrad)"/>
            <text x="210" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="27" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">75%</text>
            <text x="210" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">Use AI at work</text>
            <rect x="350" y="152" width="120" height="80" rx="8" fill="url(#gapgrad)" opacity="0.72"/>
            <text x="410" y="142" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="27" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">39%</text>
            <text x="410" y="252" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="14" fill="#847B6B">Got any AI training</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">Source: Microsoft and LinkedIn, 2024 Work Trend Index. 75% of knowledge workers use AI at work; only 39% of workplace AI users have received any AI training.</p>
        </figure>

        <div class="cav">Why it is worth it</div>
        <h2 id="matters">Why this matters even if you are not in the EU</h2>
        <p>Two reasons. First, EU digital rules have a habit of setting the global baseline: GDPR reshaped privacy laws from California to Brazil, and analysts expect the AI Act to exert similar pull, though how far is still debated. Second, and more concretely, AI literacy already pays. PwC's analysis of close to a billion job ads found a 56% wage premium for roles requiring AI skills (<a href="https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/news-room/press-releases/2025/ai-linked-to-a-fourfold-increase-in-productivity-growth.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PwC, 2025</a>), and the World Economic Forum expects 39% of workers' core skills to change by 2030 (<a href="https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-future-of-jobs-report-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WEF, 2025</a>). Whether or not the law reaches you, the skill does.</p>
        <blockquote>The EU made AI literacy a duty for employers. <span class="accent">The job market is quietly making it a duty for everyone else.</span></blockquote>

        <div class="cav">Your turn</div>
        <h2 id="find">So where do you actually stand?</h2>
        <p>The law expects AI literacy but offers no way to measure your own. The free AI Knowledge Test does, as a private self-check. It serves 50 questions across five domains, scores each objectively, and shows your level plus a radar of your strengths and gaps. To be clear: it is a personal gauge, not a certificate or proof of compliance with any law.</p>
        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">A private self-check, not a credential</span>
          <h3>Take the free AI Knowledge Test</h3>
          <p>50 questions, about 20 minutes, no signup and no email. Your level and domain radar are computed in your browser and never uploaded.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/knowledge-center/ai-knowledge-test/">Start the free test</a>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
        <h2 id="faq">Common questions about the EU AI literacy rule</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <div class="qa">
              <div class="q" set:html={f.q}></div>
              <div class="a" set:html={f.a}></div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>European Commission, <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/ai-literacy-questions-answers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI Literacy: Questions and Answers</a>, updated 2025 (the master guidance).</li>
          <li>European Union, <a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)</a>, in force 2 February 2025.</li>
          <li>European Union, <a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions, including AI literacy)</a>, 2024.</li>
          <li>European Union, <a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU AI Act, Article 99 (penalties)</a>, 2024.</li>
          <li>European Commission, <a href="https://commission.europa.eu/news-and-media/news/simpler-digital-rules-help-eu-businesses-grow-2025-11-19_en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Digital Omnibus proposal</a>, 19 November 2025 (a proposal, not yet law).</li>
          <li>Microsoft and LinkedIn, <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/ai-at-work-is-here-now-comes-the-hard-part" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2024 Work Trend Index</a>, 2024 (75% use AI, 39% trained, 66% won't hire without AI skills).</li>
          <li>PwC, <a href="https://www.pwc.com/gx/en/news-room/press-releases/2025/ai-linked-to-a-fourfold-increase-in-productivity-growth.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025 Global AI Jobs Barometer</a>, 2025.</li>
          <li>World Economic Forum, <a href="https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-future-of-jobs-report-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Future of Jobs Report 2025</a>, 2025.</li>
        </ul>
              <RelatedPlaybooks track="aikt" currentHref={seo.url} />
